312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348050 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348050 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348050 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186347312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348050 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "feb4a3c4-e3c2-59e1-b4f8-a58de2ef0e9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348376 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186348376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9500f77-53af-537c-9030-31932253fda8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348376 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186348383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348383 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348383 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186348383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48f981b1-9aeb-5e6b-818c-36bd836ee49e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348383 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186348389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348389 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186348389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93fc1e8e-53c7-5d4c-ad22-c729f51f630d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348389 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404087 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9085b031-ef66-5fae-9905-de9a5b2e5bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404088 #edb/ref 1080 "$a=1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=-2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404088 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404088 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7df3b7c7-8ab5-56e8-8b9c-3e3f6b